Sections "COLLECTION" contain missmatched items: {'COLLECTION_SUMMARY': ["There are three sets of biological samples and each set is in three (3) biological replicates. The 3 sets of samples are as follows: The B samples are samples from prothoracic glands dissected from the 5th instar larvae and immediately processed for ecdysteroids extraction. The P set of samples are samples of prothoracic glands that were incubated in Ringer's saline for 2 hr in vitro. After the incubation, the samples of glands were removed from the medium and processed for ecdysteroids extraction. The incubation medium (Ringer's saline) was also collected after the 2 hr incubation and processed immediately for ecdysteroids extraction. This last set of samples is called the S (Secreted) samples.", "There are three sets of biological samples and each set is in three (3) biological replicates. The 3 sets of samples are as follows: The B samples are samples from prothoracic glands dissected from the 5th instar larvae and immediately processed for ecdysteroids extraction. The P set of samples are samples of prothoracic glands that were incubated in Ringer''s saline for 2 hr in vitro. After the incubation, the samples of glands were removed from the medium and processed for ecdysteroids extraction. The incubation medium (Ringer''s saline) was also collected after the 2 hr incubation and processed immediately for ecdysteroids extraction. This last set of samples is called the S (Secreted) samples."]}
Sections "PROJECT" contain missmatched items: {'PROJECT_SUMMARY': ["Ecdysteroidogenic cells synthesise and secrete ecdysone, the major developmental hormone of insects. In some insect species ecdysteroidogenic cells form a homogeneous organ called the prothoracic gland. Through a complex series of enzymatic reactions carried out by enzymes encoded by the Halloween genes, cholesterol is converted to ecdysone which is then secreted to orchestrate the developmental programme of ecdysis and metamorphosis. The biosynthetic pathway of ecdysone is based on a proposed model that accounts for the presence of multiple enzymes all expressed exclusively by these cells. Although long considered as synthesizing and secreting only ecdysone, the precise mechanism of ecdysone biosynthesis by the ecdysteroidogenic cells remains unknown. More precisely, the prothoracic glands (ecdysteroidogenic cells) of the silkworm, Bombyx mori, were isolated on Day 6 of the 5th instar (D-V-6) and three sets of samples were produced. Those Before (termed B) incubation for 2 hrs in Ringer's saline, those After (termed P) incubation for 2 hrs in Ringer's saline and the incubation medium (Ringer’s saline (termed S) after 2 hrs of incubation. All these three sets of samples were done in 3 biological replicates (n=3). A targeted ecdysteroids analysis was carried out in a variety of analytical platforms using synthesized and commercially available ecdysteroids. A set of 40 ecdysteroids standards was used as reference standards in LC-MS?MS instrumentation such as Orbitrap, SciEx 6500+ and TIMS-TOFPro2, Agilent Q-TOF and Agilent TQ. The analysis identified very few of the ecdysteroids standards, an indication that ecdysteroidogenesis by the ecdysteroidogenic cells of this insect species proceeds in a way unrelated to the proposed model of ecdysteroidogenesis in insects.", "Ecdysteroidogenic cells synthesise and secrete ecdysone, the major developmental hormone of insects. In some insect species ecdysteroidogenic cells form a homogeneous organ called the prothoracic gland. Through a complex series of enzymatic reactions carried out by enzymes encoded by the Halloween genes, cholesterol is converted to ecdysone which is then secreted to orchestrate the developmental programme of ecdysis and metamorphosis. The biosynthetic pathway of ecdysone is based on a proposed model that accounts for the presence of multiple enzymes all expressed exclusively by these cells. Although long considered as synthesizing and secreting only ecdysone, the precise mechanism of ecdysone biosynthesis by the ecdysteroidogenic cells remains unknown. More precisely, the prothoracic glands (ecdysteroidogenic cells) of the silkworm, Bombyx mori, were isolated on Day 6 of the 5th instar (D-V-6) and three sets of samples were produced. Those Before (termed B) incubation for 2 hrs in Ringer''s saline, those After (termed P) incubation for 2 hrs in Ringer''s saline and the incubation medium (Ringer’s saline (termed S) after 2 hrs of incubation. All these three sets of samples were done in 3 biological replicates (n=3). A targeted ecdysteroids analysis was carried out in a variety of analytical platforms using synthesized and commercially available ecdysteroids. A set of 40 ecdysteroids standards was used as reference standards in LC-MS?MS instrumentation such as Orbitrap, SciEx 6500+ and TIMS-TOFPro2, Agilent Q-TOF and Agilent TQ. The analysis identified very few of the ecdysteroids standards, an indication that ecdysteroidogenesis by the ecdysteroidogenic cells of this insect species proceeds in a way unrelated to the proposed model of ecdysteroidogenesis in insects."]}
